          "scene_009": {
            "scene_id": "scene_009",
            "heading": "INT. MOM'S APARTMENT, BRONZEVILLE \u2014 EVENING",
            "location": "MOM'S APARTMENT, BRONZEVILLE \u2014 EVENING",
            "time_of_day": "",
            "characters": [
              "MOM",
              "MAYA"
            ],
            "text": "Maya visits her mother. Her mother notices she is different.\nMOM\nWhat?\nMAYA\nNothing.\nMOM\nYou have been staring at me forever.MAYA\nI just forgot what it felt like to see you.\nMom sets down her tea. She looks at her daughter.\nMOM\nBaby. What's going on with you?\nMAYA\nI need you to trust me about something.\nMOM\nI always trust you.\nMAYA\nEven if I can't explain it yet.\nA long beat. Mom reaches across the table and covers Maya's hand with hers.\nMOM\nWhere do you need me to go?\nMaya exhales for the first time in weeks.\nDRE NOTICES",
            "description": "Maya visits her mother. Her mother notices she is different.\nMOM\nWhat?\nMAYA\nNothing.\nMOM\nYou have been staring at me forever.MAYA\nI just forgot what it felt like to see you.\nMom sets down her tea. She looks at her daughter.\nMOM\nBaby. What's going on with you?\nMAYA\nI need you to trust me about something.\nMOM\nI always trust you.\nMAYA\nEven if I can't explain it yet.\nA long beat. Mom reaches across the table and covers Maya's hand with hers.\nMOM\nWhere do you need me to go?\nMaya exhales for the first time in weeks.\nDRE NOTICES",
            "emotional_tone": ""
          },
          "scene_010": {
            "scene_id": "scene_010",
            "heading": "INT. DRE'S APARTMENT \u2014 DAY",
            "location": "DRE'S APARTMENT \u2014 DAY",
            "time_of_day": "",
            "characters": [
              "MAYA",
              "DRE"
            ],
            "text": "Dre notices Maya changing.\nYou don't trust me anymore.\nI trust what people show me.\n\nAnd what did I show you?\nDRE\nMAYA\nDRE\nMAYA\nEnough.\nDRE\n(frustrated)\nThat's not an answer, Maya.\nMAYA\nIt's the only one I have right now.\nShe leaves. Dre watches her go. Something is working behind his eyes \u2014 not\nsuspicion, not anger. Something closer to fear.\nTHE WEIGHT OF KNOWINGINT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 ROOFTOP \u2014 NIGHT\nMaya is alone. The city is still lit below her. Chicago in its last weeks of normal.\nShe has Dre's address written on a piece of paper. She's been carrying it for days. She\ncould warn him. She could bring him in. She could also make sure he never has the\nchance to do what he did.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nI thought about it. I won't pretend I didn't. Every\nversion of what I could do to him before he did it to\nme. And I understood \u2014 that feeling. I understood Dre\nmore in that moment than I ever had.\nShe stands at the roof's edge. City lights below.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nThat's what scarcity does. It makes the math feel\nsimple. It makes monsters feel like realists. And I was\nstanding right at the edge of the same math.\nShe crumples the paper. She doesn't throw it away. She puts it back in her pocket.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nI put it away because I realized \u2014 I wasn't given this\nsecond life to become what killed me. I was given it to\nprove there was another way.\nTHE LAST NORMAL DAYS",
            "description": "Dre notices Maya changing.\nYou don't trust me anymore.\nI trust what people show me.\n\nAnd what did I show you?\nDRE\nMAYA\nDRE\nMAYA\nEnough.\nDRE\n(frustrated)\nThat's not an answer, Maya.\nMAYA\nIt's the only one I have right now.\nShe leaves. Dre watches her go. Something is working behind his eyes \u2014 not\nsuspicion, not anger. Something closer to fear.\nTHE WEIGHT OF KNOWINGINT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 ROOFTOP \u2014 NIGHT\nMaya is alone. The city is still lit below her. Chicago in its last weeks of normal.\nShe has Dre's address written on a piece of paper. She's been carrying it for days. She\ncould warn him. She could bring him in. She could also make sure he never has the\nchance to do what he did.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nI thought about it. I won't pretend I didn't. Every\nversion of what I could do to him before he did it to\nme. And I understood \u2014 that feeling. I understood Dre\nmore in that moment than I ever had.\nShe stands at the roof's edge. City lights below.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nThat's what scarcity does. It makes the math feel\nsimple. It makes monsters feel like realists. And I was\nstanding right at the edge of the same math.\nShe crumples the paper. She doesn't throw it away. She puts it back in her pocket.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nI put it away because I realized \u2014 I wasn't given this\nsecond life to become what killed me. I was given it to\nprove there was another way.\nTHE LAST NORMAL DAYS",
            "emotional_tone": ""
          },
          "scene_011": {
            "scene_id": "scene_011",
            "heading": "EXT. CHICAGO \u2014 VARIOUS LOCATIONS \u2014 DAY",
            "location": "CHICAGO \u2014 VARIOUS LOCATIONS \u2014 DAY",
            "time_of_day": "",
            "characters": [],
            "text": "Chicago is still beautiful. People celebrate. People laugh. Maya moves through it all\nlike she's memorizing it. Every storefront. Every face. Every ordinary miracle.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nPeople ask what you'd do differently if you knew. I\nknow now. You'd stop skipping the small things. You'd\nstay at the table longer.\nTHE APOCALYPSE BEGINS\nThe first blackout happens. Then another. The city changes. The hospitals collapse.\nThe streets become dangerous. Stores empty. People fight for resources. Chicago\nbecomes unrecognizable.\nBut Maya's homestead works. The building comes alive. Her preparation becomes\nreality.\nThe cause: a cascading infrastructure failure \u2014 power grid, then supply chains, then\ncommunications. Not one event. A system that was already failing, finally failing all at\nonce.THE HOMESTEAD COMMUNITY",
            "description": "Chicago is still beautiful. People celebrate. People laugh. Maya moves through it all\nlike she's memorizing it. Every storefront. Every face. Every ordinary miracle.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nPeople ask what you'd do differently if you knew. I\nknow now. You'd stop skipping the small things. You'd\nstay at the table longer.\nTHE APOCALYPSE BEGINS\nThe first blackout happens. Then another. The city changes. The hospitals collapse.\nThe streets become dangerous. Stores empty. People fight for resources. Chicago\nbecomes unrecognizable.\nBut Maya's homestead works. The building comes alive. Her preparation becomes\nreality.\nThe cause: a cascading infrastructure failure \u2014 power grid, then supply chains, then\ncommunications. Not one event. A system that was already failing, finally failing all at\nonce.THE HOMESTEAD COMMUNITY",
            "emotional_tone": ""
          },
          "scene_012": {
            "scene_id": "scene_012",
            "heading": "INT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 VARIOUS \u2014 DAY",
            "location": "THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 VARIOUS \u2014 DAY",
            "time_of_day": "",
            "characters": [],
            "text": "Inside are Maya's chosen survivors. Her mother. Jay. Simone. Elena. Others she\ntrusted. The building has food. Water. Power. Life.\nSimone runs the medical floor. Jay keeps the power on. Elena tends the rooftop farm.\nMaya's mother moves through all of it \u2014 feeding people, keeping rhythm, being the\nwarmth the building needs.\nOutside, the city continues falling apart.\nA MEAL SAVED FOR",
