            "text": "Late light through a thin curtain. A single pot of water works toward a boil. On the\ncounter: a small, careful inventory \u2014 spaghetti, a few fillets of catfish, one tomato,\ngarlic going soft at the root. Portions stretched carefully to reach four.\nMom stands at the stove, sleeves pushed up. Maya leans in the doorway a beat before\nshe moves \u2014 watching before joining, the way she does.\nMOM\n(not turning around)\nYou gonna help, or you gonna haunt the doorway all\nnight.\nMaya smiles despite herself. Crosses in. Picks up the garlic without being asked.\nClose on hands: Maya peeling garlic, the papery skin coming away in small curls. Mom\nsalting the water \u2014 a practiced toss, more feel than measurement.\nMAYA\nWe're really doing the catfish? For all of us?\nMOM\nBeen saving it. Tonight's a good night for it.\nA beat. Maya doesn't ask why tonight. She understands it without needing to.\nMom sets up her station with quiet ritual \u2014 a shallow dish of cornmeal and flour,\nseasoned heavy; a bowl of buttermilk gone slightly warm; a deep, heavy pot with oil\ncreeping up its sides. She drags a fingertip through the oil's surface, waits, watches for\nthe shimmer.\nThe catfish fillets soak in the buttermilk \u2014 Mom turning each one over with her fingers,\ncoating them fully. One by one, she lifts them dripping and presses them into the\ncornmeal, turning, pressing again, making sure every edge is caught.\nMaya drops the spaghetti into the boiling pot \u2014 more of it than usual, enough to\nmatter. Stirs. The kitchen fills with steam from her side and, from Mom's, the low tick\nand pop of oil coming up to heat.Mom tests the oil with a pinch of cornmeal flicked in \u2014 it sizzles and rises\nimmediately. Satisfied, she lowers the first fillet in with careful hands, then the next,\nthen the next. The oil roars up around them, a wall of sound and rising gold bubbles.\nA long moment with no dialogue. Just: the churn of frying oil. The soft roll of boiling\nwater. Wooden spoon against metal. Golden evening light thickening to amber across\nthe counter, catching the steam and the oil's haze like it's lit from within.\nMom watches the fillets with total attention now \u2014 nudging them gently so they don't\nstick to the bottom, listening for the sound to change, the color to deepen. She lifts\none at the edge with a slotted spoon, checks the color, lowers it back.\nMaya watches her mother's hands \u2014 the sureness of them, built from doing this with\nless than they needed, and now stretching it further still.\nMAYA\n(quiet)\nIt smells like it used to.\nMom glances at her \u2014 just a flicker, something tender and unspoken passing between\nthem. She doesn't look away from the pot for long.\nOne by one, she lifts the fillets out, deep gold and crackling, onto a plate lined with\npaper towel. The oil hisses and settles as each one comes free.\nMOM\nTaste this.\nShe tears a small corner off one fillet, blows on it, hands it to Maya. Maya takes it,\nbites, and for a second just chews \u2014 eyes closing, something like relief crossing her\nface.\nMAYA\nPerfect.\nMOM\n(small, satisfied)\nCourse it is.\nThey plate the food together \u2014 four bowls of spaghetti, a piece of crackling catfish laid\nacross each, careful that no one's portion looks smaller than another's.\nThey carry the bowls out toward the others \u2014 Jay, Elena, Simone waiting, low light\ncatching the steam rising off four plates instead of two.\nBowls set down one by one. No ceremony, just the quiet efficiency of people who've\nlearned to move around each other in small spaces. Maya hands Jay his plate; Elena\nscoots over to make room; Simone's already reaching for a fork before hers even\nlands.\nFor a moment, no one eats. Everyone around a table built for fewer \u2014 Mom the last to\nsit, wiping her hands on her apron before finally lowering into her chair.A beat of stillness. Steam curling upward, catching the last of the amber light.\nThen, without a word, they all start at once \u2014 the small clatter of forks, the first bite.\nJay closes his eyes over the fish the way Maya did earlier. He says something too\nquiet to hear, and Elena laughs, soft and real.\nMom watches them eat before touching her own plate \u2014 the same look she gave\nMaya earlier, multiplied across the table.\nHold on the group \u2014 heads bent over their bowls, the room lit gold, the ones who\nmade it this far, eating like it's nothing and like it's everything.\nFINAL CONFRONTATION\nEXT./INT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 DAY\nDre finds the building. He looks rough \u2014 hollowed out, hungry. But his eyes are still\nsharp. He sees everything.\nThe lights. The gardens. The survivors. He sees Maya's mother alive through a\nwindow.\nHe understands she knew.\nDRE\nHow?\nMaya says nothing.\nDRE\nHow did you know?\nMAYA\nI had a nightmare.\nDRE\nA nightmare.\nMAYA\nThe kind you wake up from different.\nDRE\n(quiet, not accusatory \u2014 just broken)\nYou could have saved us.\nMAYA\nI tried.\nDRE\nWhen?\nMAYA\nBefore you became the person who needed saving.A long silence. Dre looks at the building \u2014 the life inside it. Then back at Maya.\nDRE\n(barely)\nscared.\nI was scared. I want you to know that. I was so\nMAYA\nI know.\nDRE\nDoes it matter?\nMAYA\n(beat)\nIt matters that you said it.\nMaya gives Dre supplies. A map. A chance. But not her sanctuary. Not her future. She\nwatches him go. She doesn't look away until he's gone.\nENDING",
