          {
            "scene_id": "scene_003",
            "intent": "He's cooking. Music playing. The apartment feels lived-in, warm.\nShe watches him from the doorway before he notices her.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nThis is the version of him I want to remember. The\none who used to split his last twenty dollars with me at\nthe gas station. The one who showed up when I\nneeded him. That person was real. I have to believe\nthat.\nDre turns and sees her.\nDRE\nYou good? You're looking at me weird.\nMAYA\nI'm fine. Just tired.\nDRE\nYou eat?\nMAYA\nYeah.\nHe puts a plate in front of her anyway. She looks down at it. She knows that in two\nmonths this apartment will be stripped bare. She knows what hunger will do to him.\nMAYA\nHey. If something happened \u2014 something bad \u2014\nyou'd tell me, right?\nDRE\nWhat kind of something?\nMAYA\nI don't know. Anything. Would you come to me?\nDRE\n(beat, easy smile)\nMaya. You're my people. Always.\nShe nods. She almost believes him.\nTHE COUNTDOWN\nMaya creates a survival blueprint. Food. Water. Power. Medical. Security. People.\nShe writes two lists.\nThe first list: names of people she will save.\nThe second list: names she cannot. Dre's name is not on either list yet.She stares at both lists for a long time. Then she folds them and puts them away.\nTHE HOMESTEAD\nEXT./INT. ABANDONED HIGH RISE, DOWNTOWN CHICAGO \u2014 DAY\nMaya finds an abandoned high rise in Downtown Chicago. A building everyone forgot.\nShe sees a future inside it. She transforms it into a survival sanctuary.\nThe rooftop becomes a farm. Hydroponics. Seeds. Rain collection. Winter preparation.\nThe building gets solar power, batteries, generators, storage rooms, a medical area,\nsecurity systems, and emergency exits.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nI'm not preparing for monsters. I'm preparing for\npeople. People are always the hardest part.\nSUPPLIES\nMaya collects supplies before the world knows it needs them. Food. Water filters.\nMedicine. Tools. Seeds. Preservation equipment. Repair materials.\nShe learns how to maintain everything. Every decision matters because she knows\ntime is running out.\nTHE SUPPLY RUN \u2014 WHAT GOES WRONG",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_004",
            "intent": "Maya is mid-run. She's loaded two carts of medical supplies from an overlooked\npharmaceutical warehouse. She's almost to the loading dock when she hears voices.\nThree men. Armed. Not police. Scavengers \u2014 before the scavenging has even\nofficially started.\nShe freezes. One cart tips. A glass bottle shatters on the concrete floor.\nThe men look toward the sound.\nMaya abandons one cart. She pushes the other into the dark and holds her breath\nagainst a pillar while they sweep the room with flashlights.\nShe gets out. But she lost half the antibiotics she came for.",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_005",
            "intent": "She sits on the floor. Looks at what she saved. Then at the list of what she lost.\nMAYA\n(to herself)\nNot enough. It's never going to be enough.\nShe stays there for a moment. Then she gets back up. She writes a new list. Different\nwarehouses. Different approach. She adapts.THE CHOSEN \u2014 RECRUITMENT",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_006",
            "intent": "SIMONE, 40s, a trauma nurse with twelve-hour circles under her eyes, is eating alone.\nMaya sits across from her uninvited.\nMAYA\nYou worked the West Side clinic flood response in '19.\nSIMONE\n(without looking up)\nDo I know you?\nMAYA\nNot yet. I need someone who can work with almost\nnothing and keep people alive.\nSIMONE\nThat's my whole job.\nMAYA\nThen this should feel familiar.\nSimone finally looks up. Something in Maya's tone cuts through the exhaustion.",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_007",
            "intent": "JAY, 30s, Maya's cousin \u2014 quiet, meticulous \u2014 is mid-repair on an old generator. He's\nthe kind of person who fixes things not because he has to but because broken things\nbother him.\nMaya leans against the bay door.\nMAYA\nI need you to look at something.\nJAY\nWhere?\nMAYA\nBuilding downtown. Off the grid.\nJAY\nWhat for?\nMAYA\nI need to know if the solar setup will hold through a\nChicago winter.\nJAY\n(wipes his hands on a rag)\nWhat are you building, Maya?MAYA\nSomething that lasts.\nHe follows her.",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_008",
            "intent": "ELENA, 60s, is tending rows of winter kale. She doesn't stop working when Maya\napproaches.\nELENA\nYou the one who's been asking about hydroponics at\nthe library?\nMAYA\nYes.\nELENA\nYou don't have soil. So you're growing indoors.\nMAYA\nRooftop. Maybe indoors eventually.\nELENA\n(still not looking up)\nWhy?\nMAYA\nBecause I think the stores are going to stop.\nElena pauses. She looks at Maya for a long moment.\nELENA\nYou're not the first person to feel that.\nMAYA\nI might be the first one doing something about it.\nElena sets down her trowel.\nMAYA AND HER MOM",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_009",
            "intent": "Maya visits her mother. Her mother notices she is different.\nMOM\nWhat?\nMAYA\nNothing.\nMOM\nYou have been staring at me forever.MAYA\nI just forgot what it felt like to see you.\nMom sets down her tea. She looks at her daughter.\nMOM\nBaby. What's going on with you?\nMAYA\nI need you to trust me about something.\nMOM\nI always trust you.\nMAYA\nEven if I can't explain it yet.\nA long beat. Mom reaches across the table and covers Maya's hand with hers.\nMOM\nWhere do you need me to go?\nMaya exhales for the first time in weeks.\nDRE NOTICES",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_010",
            "intent": "Dre notices Maya changing.\nYou don't trust me anymore.\nI trust what people show me.\n\nAnd what did I show you?\nDRE\nMAYA\nDRE\nMAYA\nEnough.\nDRE\n(frustrated)\nThat's not an answer, Maya.\nMAYA\nIt's the only one I have right now.\nShe leaves. Dre watches her go. Something is working behind his eyes \u2014 not\nsuspicion, not anger. Something closer to fear.\nTHE WEIGHT OF KNOWINGINT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 ROOFTOP \u2014 NIGHT\nMaya is alone. The city is still lit below her. Chicago in its last weeks of normal.\nShe has Dre's address written on a piece of paper. She's been carrying it for days. She\ncould warn him. She could bring him in. She could also make sure he never has the\nchance to do what he did.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nI thought about it. I won't pretend I didn't. Every\nversion of what I could do to him before he did it to\nme. And I understood \u2014 that feeling. I understood Dre\nmore in that moment than I ever had.\nShe stands at the roof's edge. City lights below.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nThat's what scarcity does. It makes the math feel\nsimple. It makes monsters feel like realists. And I was\nstanding right at the edge of the same math.\nShe crumples the paper. She doesn't throw it away. She puts it back in her pocket.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nI put it away because I realized \u2014 I wasn't given this\nsecond life to become what killed me. I was given it to\nprove there was another way.\nTHE LAST NORMAL DAYS",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_011",
            "intent": "Chicago is still beautiful. People celebrate. People laugh. Maya moves through it all\nlike she's memorizing it. Every storefront. Every face. Every ordinary miracle.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nPeople ask what you'd do differently if you knew. I\nknow now. You'd stop skipping the small things. You'd\nstay at the table longer.\nTHE APOCALYPSE BEGINS\nThe first blackout happens. Then another. The city changes. The hospitals collapse.\nThe streets become dangerous. Stores empty. People fight for resources. Chicago\nbecomes unrecognizable.\nBut Maya's homestead works. The building comes alive. Her preparation becomes\nreality.\nThe cause: a cascading infrastructure failure \u2014 power grid, then supply chains, then\ncommunications. Not one event. A system that was already failing, finally failing all at\nonce.THE HOMESTEAD COMMUNITY",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_012",
            "intent": "Inside are Maya's chosen survivors. Her mother. Jay. Simone. Elena. Others she\ntrusted. The building has food. Water. Power. Life.\nSimone runs the medical floor. Jay keeps the power on. Elena tends the rooftop farm.\nMaya's mother moves through all of it \u2014 feeding people, keeping rhythm, being the\nwarmth the building needs.\nOutside, the city continues falling apart.\nA MEAL SAVED FOR",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_013",
            "intent": "Late light through a thin curtain. A single pot of water works toward a boil. On the\ncounter: a small, careful inventory \u2014 spaghetti, a few fillets of catfish, one tomato,\ngarlic going soft at the root. Portions stretched carefully to reach four.\nMom stands at the stove, sleeves pushed up. Maya leans in the doorway a beat before\nshe moves \u2014 watching before joining, the way she does.\nMOM\n(not turning around)\nYou gonna help, or you gonna haunt the doorway all\nnight.\nMaya smiles despite herself. Crosses in. Picks up the garlic without being asked.\nClose on hands: Maya peeling garlic, the papery skin coming away in small curls. Mom\nsalting the water \u2014 a practiced toss, more feel than measurement.\nMAYA\nWe're really doing the catfish? For all of us?\nMOM\nBeen saving it. Tonight's a good night for it.\nA beat. Maya doesn't ask why tonight. She understands it without needing to.\nMom sets up her station with quiet ritual \u2014 a shallow dish of cornmeal and flour,\nseasoned heavy; a bowl of buttermilk gone slightly warm; a deep, heavy pot with oil\ncreeping up its sides. She drags a fingertip through the oil's surface, waits, watches for\nthe shimmer.\nThe catfish fillets soak in the buttermilk \u2014 Mom turning each one over with her fingers,\ncoating them fully. One by one, she lifts them dripping and presses them into the\ncornmeal, turning, pressing again, making sure every edge is caught.\nMaya drops the spaghetti into the boiling pot \u2014 more of it than usual, enough to\nmatter. Stirs. The kitchen fills with steam from her side and, from Mom's, the low tick\nand pop of oil coming up to heat.Mom tests the oil with a pinch of cornmeal flicked in \u2014 it sizzles and rises\nimmediately. Satisfied, she lowers the first fillet in with careful hands, then the next,\nthen the next. The oil roars up around them, a wall of sound and rising gold bubbles.\nA long moment with no dialogue. Just: the churn of frying oil. The soft roll of boiling\nwater. Wooden spoon against metal. Golden evening light thickening to amber across\nthe counter, catching the steam and the oil's haze like it's lit from within.\nMom watches the fillets with total attention now \u2014 nudging them gently so they don't\nstick to the bottom, listening for the sound to change, the color to deepen. She lifts\none at the edge with a slotted spoon, checks the color, lowers it back.\nMaya watches her mother's hands \u2014 the sureness of them, built from doing this with\nless than they needed, and now stretching it further still.\nMAYA\n(quiet)\nIt smells like it used to.\nMom glances at her \u2014 just a flicker, something tender and unspoken passing between\nthem. She doesn't look away from the pot for long.\nOne by one, she lifts the fillets out, deep gold and crackling, onto a plate lined with\npaper towel. The oil hisses and settles as each one comes free.\nMOM\nTaste this.\nShe tears a small corner off one fillet, blows on it, hands it to Maya. Maya takes it,\nbites, and for a second just chews \u2014 eyes closing, something like relief crossing her\nface.\nMAYA\nPerfect.\nMOM\n(small, satisfied)\nCourse it is.\nThey plate the food together \u2014 four bowls of spaghetti, a piece of crackling catfish laid\nacross each, careful that no one's portion looks smaller than another's.\nThey carry the bowls out toward the others \u2014 Jay, Elena, Simone waiting, low light\ncatching the steam rising off four plates instead of two.\nBowls set down one by one. No ceremony, just the quiet efficiency of people who've\nlearned to move around each other in small spaces. Maya hands Jay his plate; Elena\nscoots over to make room; Simone's already reaching for a fork before hers even\nlands.\nFor a moment, no one eats. Everyone around a table built for fewer \u2014 Mom the last to\nsit, wiping her hands on her apron before finally lowering into her chair.A beat of stillness. Steam curling upward, catching the last of the amber light.\nThen, without a word, they all start at once \u2014 the small clatter of forks, the first bite.\nJay closes his eyes over the fish the way Maya did earlier. He says something too\nquiet to hear, and Elena laughs, soft and real.\nMom watches them eat before touching her own plate \u2014 the same look she gave\nMaya earlier, multiplied across the table.\nHold on the group \u2014 heads bent over their bowls, the room lit gold, the ones who\nmade it this far, eating like it's nothing and like it's everything.\nFINAL CONFRONTATION\nEXT./INT. THE HOMESTEAD \u2014 DAY\nDre finds the building. He looks rough \u2014 hollowed out, hungry. But his eyes are still\nsharp. He sees everything.\nThe lights. The gardens. The survivors. He sees Maya's mother alive through a\nwindow.\nHe understands she knew.\nDRE\nHow?\nMaya says nothing.\nDRE\nHow did you know?\nMAYA\nI had a nightmare.\nDRE\nA nightmare.\nMAYA\nThe kind you wake up from different.\nDRE\n(quiet, not accusatory \u2014 just broken)\nYou could have saved us.\nMAYA\nI tried.\nDRE\nWhen?\nMAYA\nBefore you became the person who needed saving.A long silence. Dre looks at the building \u2014 the life inside it. Then back at Maya.\nDRE\n(barely)\nscared.\nI was scared. I want you to know that. I was so\nMAYA\nI know.\nDRE\nDoes it matter?\nMAYA\n(beat)\nIt matters that you said it.\nMaya gives Dre supplies. A map. A chance. But not her sanctuary. Not her future. She\nwatches him go. She doesn't look away until he's gone.\nENDING",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          },
          {
            "scene_id": "scene_014",
            "intent": "Maya stands on the rooftop. Chicago is dark. Her building glows. Plants move in the\nwind. A small piece of humanity survives.\nBelow, she can hear voices. Her mother. Jay. Simone. Elena. People living.\nMAYA (V.O.)\nThe first time the world ended, I lost everything.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nThe second time, I prepared.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nI thought being reborn meant getting revenge. I was\nwrong.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nIt meant I finally had the power to choose what\nsurvived.\nShe looks out at the dark city. At the stars finally visible without the light pollution.\nMAYA (V.O.) (CONT'D)\nThe nightmare showed me who we became. The\nsecond chance showed me who we could be instead.\nI chose that version. Every single day, I chose it.\nFADE TO BLACK.FADE OUT.",
            "linked_refs": [],
            "shot_count": 1
          }
        ],
